ing: 0px; top: 0px;display:inline;"><div style=3D"font-weight:bold;display:=
inline;">Structural Market Risk Measurement </div></div></div></div> <div s=
tyle=3D"font-family: arial; font-size: 8pt; margin-top: 6pt; margin-bottom:=
 0pt;"><div style=3D"color: rgb(34, 92, 171); letter-spacing: 0px; top: 0px=
;display:inline;"><div style=3D"letter-spacing: 0px; top: 0px;display:inlin=
e;"><div style=3D"font-weight:bold;display:inline;">Interest Rate Risk </di=
v></div></div></div> <div style=3D"font-family: arial; font-size: 8pt; marg=
in-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;"><div style=3D"color: rgb(34, 92, 171); le=
tter-spacing: 0px; top: 0px;display:inline;">Structural interest rate risk =
arises when changes in interest rates affect the market value, cash flows a=
nd earnings of assets and liabilities related to&nbsp;our banking activitie=
s. The objective of structural interest rate risk management is to maintain=
 high-quality earnings and maximize sustainable product spreads, while mana=
ging risk to the economic value of our net assets arising from changes in i=
nterest rates. </div></div> <div style=3D"text-indent: 4%; font-family: ari=
al; font-size: 8pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;"><div style=3D"col=
or: rgb(34, 92, 171); letter-spacing: 0px; top: 0px;display:inline;">Struct=
ural interest rate risk primarily comprises interest rate mismatch risk and=
 product-embedded option risk. </div></div> <div style=3D"text-indent: 4%; =
font-family: arial; font-size: 8pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;"><=
div style=3D"color: rgb(34, 92, 171); letter-spacing: 0px; top: 0px;display=
:inline;">Interest rate mismatch risk arises when there are differences in =
the scheduled maturities, repricing dates or reference rates of assets, lia=
bilities and derivatives. The net interest rate mismatch, representing resi=
dual assets funded by common shareholders=E2=80=99 equity, is managed to al=
ign with a target maturity profile through interest rate swaps and securiti=
es. </div></div> <div style=3D"text-indent: 4%; font-family: arial; font-si=
ze: 8pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;"><div style=3D"color: rgb(34,=
 92, 171); letter-spacing: 0px; top: 0px;display:inline;">Product-embedded =
option risk arises when product features allow customers to alter the timin=
g of cash flows, such as scheduled maturity or repricing dates, usually in =
response to changes in market conditions. Product-embedded options include =
loan prepayments, deposit redemption privileges and committed rates on unad=
vanced mortgages. Product-embedded options and associated customer behaviou=
r are captured in risk modelling, and hedging programs may be used to limit=
 the level of exposure to this risk. </div></div> <div style=3D"text-indent=
: 4%; font-family: arial; font-size: 8pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0=
pt;"><div style=3D"color: rgb(34, 92, 171); letter-spacing: 0px; top: 0px;d=
isplay:inline;">Structural interest rate risk is measured using simulations=
, analyses of the sensitivity of earnings and economic value, stress testin=
g and gap analysis, in addition to other risk metrics. </div></div> <div st=
yle=3D"font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;"><div style=3D=
"font-size: 10pt; letter-spacing: 0px; top: 0px;display:inline;">&nbsp;</di=
v></div> <div style=3D"padding-top: 2pt; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-right:=
 0%; margin-left: 0%; max-width: 100%; background-color: rgb(232, 238, 246)=
;"> <div style=3D"margin: 0pt 1%; padding-top: 0pt; font-family: arial; fon=
t-size: 8pt;"><div style=3D"letter-spacing: 0px; top: 0px;display:inline;">=
<div style=3D"font-weight:bold;display:inline;">Earnings Sensitivity</div><=
/div> is a measure of the impact of potential changes in interest rates on =
the projected <div style=3D"white-space: nowrap; letter-spacing: 0px; top: =
0px;display:inline;">12-month</div> <div style=3D"white-space: nowrap; lett=
er-spacing: 0px; top: 0px;display:inline;">pre-tax</div> net income from a =
portfolio of assets, liabilities and <div style=3D"white-space: nowrap; let=
ter-spacing: 0px; top: 0px;display:inline;">off-balance</div> sheet positio=
ns in response to prescribed parallel interest rate movements, with interes=
t rates floored at zero. </div> <div style=3D"margin: 6pt 1% 0pt; padding-b=
ottom: 0pt; font-family: arial; font-size: 8pt;"><div style=3D"letter-spaci=
ng: 0px; top: 0px;display:inline;"><div style=3D"font-weight:bold;display:i=
nline;">Economic Value Sensitivity</div></div> is a measure of the impact o=
f potential changes in interest rates on the market value of a portfolio of=
 assets, liabilities and <div style=3D"white-space: nowrap; letter-spacing:=
 0px; top: 0px;display:inline;">off-balance</div> sheet positions in respon=
se to prescribed parallel interest rate movements, with interest rates floo=
red at zero. </div> </div> <div style=3D"font-family: arial; font-size: 8pt=
; margin-top: 6pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;"><div style=3D"color: rgb(34, 92, 17=
1); letter-spacing: 0px; top: 0px;display:inline;">The models that measure =
structural interest rate risk incorporate projected changes in interest rat=
es and predict the likely reaction of our customers to these changes. For c=
ustomer loans and deposits with scheduled maturity and repricing dates (suc=
h as mortgages and term deposits), the models measure the extent to which c=
ustomers are likely to use embedded options to alter those scheduled dates =
and timing. For customer loans and deposits without scheduled maturity and =
repricing dates (such as credit card loans and chequing accounts), exposure=
 is measured using models that adjust for elasticity in product pricing and=
 reflect historical and forecasted trends in balances. The results generate=
d by these structural market risk models are inherently uncertain, as they =
reflect potential future pricing and customer behaviour, which may differ f=
rom actual experience. These models have been developed using statistical a=
nalysis and are independently validated and periodically updated through re=
gular model performance assessment, back-testing and ongoing dialogue with =
the lines of business. Models developed to predict customer behaviour are a=
lso used to support product pricing. </div></div> <div style=3D"font-size: =
8pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;"><div style=3D"font-size: 8pt; le=
